course: dictionary
lesson: 13338
title: Old words for clothes
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old and grand. Writers use them to sound formal, or sometimes for a joke. Habiliment is one of these words. It means clothes, especially special clothes worn for a job or a ceremony. «The king put on his royal habiliments for the crowning.» The words royal and crowning are clues that the habiliments are special, fancy clothes.
explain: Habiliments is often used in the plural, with an s. «Her habiliments were stiff with gold thread.» Clues like gold and thread point to fine clothes. To check your meaning, swap in clothes and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Be careful, because habiliment is rare and old, and most people do not use it today. «The old book described the knight's habiliments in great detail.» You do not need to know every thread or button. The clue is that the sentence is about clothing, so you know the word is about clothes.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad escapes from prison dressed as a washerwoman. Old-fashioned words like habiliment can make a disguise sound grand and funny. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Habiliment means clothes, especially special clothes worn for a job or a ceremony.
fact: Habiliments is usually the plural form, with an s, because people often mean several items of clothing.
fact: Habiliment is a rare, old and formal word, so you will mostly find it in old books.
fact: Writers sometimes use grand old words like this for a joke, because they sound so fancy.
exercise: «The knight polished his shining habiliments before the ceremony.» What are habiliments? | Shoes for walking far | *Clothes, especially special clothes | A kind of horse | Tools for polishing armour
exercise: «Her habiliments sparkled with jewels.» What does habiliments mean here? | Her hair | Her shoes | *Her clothes | Her house
exercise: Why might a writer use habiliment instead of clothes? | Because it is the most common word for clothes today | *To sound grand, old-fashioned or funny | Because it means shoes | Because it is a short word
test: «The wedding guests arrived in their finest habiliments.» Finest habiliments most likely means? | Their lunch boxes | *Their best, most special clothes | Their muddy boots | Their pets
test: Which sentence uses habiliments correctly? | The rain fell on the habiliments. | I ate my habiliments for lunch. | *The queen wore her royal habiliments. | The habiliments barked at the dog.
exam: In an old book, «The duke's habiliments were trimmed with gold.» What are habiliments? | A type of boat | A gold coin | A thin cough | *Clothes
